ABSTRACT
============================================================


Climate change has led to water shortages becoming a global issue. Many studies are
focused on finding stable, reliable, and cost-eeective alternative water sources to achieve
sustainable water supply. Wastewater recycling is one of the solutions to water shortages.
By recycling secondary and tertiary eeluent from wastewater treatment plants and
processing it through a comprehensive Full Advanced Treatment (FAT) system, it can be
used as an alternative water source for drinking water facilities.
To address the issues of low photon utilization eeiciency and residuals in UV/H2O2
advanced oxidation processes, scholars are now using UV/HOCl as a replacement.
However, HOCl undergoes breakpoint chlorination reactions with chloramines present in
the RO permeate. This study uses Kintecus software to simulate the concentration
changes of free chlorine and chloramines in RO permeate based on the mathematical
model developed by Jafvert and Valentine. The average concentration of species after 30
seconds of breakpoint chlorination is multiplied by their molar absorptivity coeeicients in
the 230-400 nm range to obtain absorbance. Simulation results show that dichloramine
has a high absorbance in the UVB range. This suggests that switching the UV/HOCl light
source to UVB wavelengths could enhance the eeiciency of the UV advanced oxidation
process in degrading trace pollutants.
In this study, laboratory-synthesized RO permeate was spiked with six model
micropollutants: nitrobenzene, benzoic acid, carbamazepine, ca0eine, 1,4-dioxane,
and N,N-diethyl-m-toluamide. The objective was to investigate whether UVB306
nm/breakpoint chlorination can eeectively transfer energy to dichloramine, thereby
generating free radicals more eeiciently and enhancing pollutant degradation. Finally, the
study further explored optimizing the operating conditions of UVB306 nm/breakpoint
chlorination advanced oxidation processes (AOP) by examining the eeects of a 90-second
dark reaction post-chlorination before irradiation, reducing the energy output per unit time
of the UVB306 nm lamp, and changing the reactor's reflective surface material.
Additionally, the study examined the formation and degradation of N-nitroso
dimethylamine (NDMA).
The study found that the degradation eeiciency of pollutants was optimal using a 4 W
UVB306 nm/breakpoint chlorination system in a Teflon reactor. Compared to using an 8 W
UVC254 nm/H2O2 and an 8 W UVC254 nm/breakpoint chlorination in a stainless steel
reactor, the degradation eeiciencies for nitrobenzene and 1,4-dioxane were improved by


73% and 29%, respectively. In the degradation of NDMA, the 4 W UVB306 nm/breakpoint
chlorination with a Teflon reactor showed promising results. For countries facing water
scarcity, the UVB with breakpoint chlorination technique in advanced oxidation processes
holds potential. Future studies need to ensure that the treated eeluent does not exhibit
higher toxicity.
Keywords: UVB306 nm/breakpoint chlorination, synthetic RO permeate, model
micropollutant, absorbance, N-nitroso dimethylamine
