CHAPTER 1. RESEARCH BACKGROUND
============================================================
1.1 Research Background and Motivation
Environmental change and climate change, population growth, and economic
development have caused water scarcity to become a global problem. Taiwan, California in
the United States, Australia, and Singapore all face water shortages. In California, for
example, climate change has caused the snowpack to gradually decrease (Ullrich et al.,
2018). Therefore, finding stable, reliable, and cost-eeective alternative water sources is
currently a major direction and goal of research.
Wastewater recycling is part of the concept of sustainable water supply and "closing the
water loop," and is one means of addressing water scarcity. In California, secondary and
tertiary eeluent from municipal wastewater treatment plants is collected and treated
through a full advanced treatment (FAT) system (Soufan et al., 2013). After stabilization, the
treated water can be supplied to drinking-water plants as an alternative source (Gerrity et
al., 2013; Kwon et al., 2020).
As shown in Figure 1-1, the FAT system first adds hypochlorous acid (HOCl) before
membrane treatment processes such as microfiltration (MF), ultrafiltration (UF), and
reverse osmosis (RO) to inhibit biological growth on the membranes (Wang et al., 2021).
Because secondary and tertiary eeluents contain some ammonia nitrogen, added HOCl
reacts with ammonia nitrogen and forms chloramine species, mainly monochloramine and
dichloramine. Because chloramine species are small neutral molecules that are not
easily removed by membranes (Gerrity et al., 2013), they ultimately remain in the RO
permeate and enter the UV reactor. The conventional approach is to use H2O2 as the
oxidant for advanced oxidation treatment in the UV reactor.


Figure 1-1. FAT treatment flow for wastewater reuse. Source: Gerrity et al. (2013).
Although UV/H2O2 is widely used in water-reuse treatment systems internationally, it has
two major problems. First, at 254 nm, chloramine species have much higher molar
absorptivity than the 18.6 M^-1 cm^-1 of H2O2 (Yin et al., 2020). If the background matrix
contains a certain concentration of chloramines, most of the UV energy emitted into
the water is absorbed by chloramines, leaving very little energy for H2O2. Studies
indicate that only about 10% of H2O2 consumed in a UV/H2O2 AOP is used to remove
pollutants in water (Gao et al., 2020). Second, before treated water enters an
environmental bueer water body, post-chlorination is required to maintain disinfection. To
maintain the residual chlorine concentration, additional HOCl must be added to react with
and remove the remaining H2O2 (Equation 1.1) (Guo et al., 2017; Kwon et al., 2020; Roback
et al., 2021; Wu et al., 2016).
HOCl + H2O2 -> O2 + H2O + Cl- + H+ (1.1)
To address the low photon-utilization eeiciency and residual-oxidant problems of
UV/H2O2, many researchers over the past decade have studied UV/HOCl or UV/chloramine
as alternatives. Kwon et al. (2020) and Roback et al. (2021), for example, investigated RO
permeate from the Orange County Water District Groundwater Replenishment System
(OCWD/GWRS) and examined practical applications of UV/HOCl and UV/chloramine in
place of UV/H2O2.
Because chloramines are inevitably present at some concentration in RO permeate from
water-reuse systems, when the advanced oxidation step after the RO membrane is
changed to UV/HOCl, HOCl reacts with chloramines and, at certain chlorine-to-nitrogen
molar ratios, produces a breakpoint chlorination reaction. The final forms produced by the
reaction between HOCl and chloramines depend mainly on the molar ratio of free chlorine
(HOCl and OCl-) to ammonia and the reaction time (Aghdam et al., 2021).
In general, under neutral conditions, breakpoint chlorination reaches a stable state
approximately 20-30 minutes after the reaction begins and can be divided into three stages


(Figure 1-2). Before a chlorine-to-nitrogen mass ratio of 5:1 (approximately Cl2/N molar =
1:1), when free chlorine is added to water containing ammonia nitrogen, free chlorine
undergoes a series of complex reactions with ammonia to form combined residual
chlorine, primarily monochloramine. As the amount of free chlorine increases, combined
residual chlorine and total chlorine concentrations also increase. When Cl2/N molar
reaches 1:1, free chlorine begins consuming the combined residual chlorine until the
breakpoint is reached, at which point Cl2/N molar is approximately 1.5:1-2:1 (Metcalf et
al., 2007; Wahman, 2018).
The breakpoint is the point at which the total chlorine concentration in the water is lowest.
Before the breakpoint, dichloramine is the major species. When the chlorine-to-nitrogen
molar ratio exceeds the breakpoint, the combined residual chlorine has been consumed
and further addition of free chlorine remains in the water as free residual chlorine (Jafvert
and Valentine, 1992).
Figure 1-2. Breakpoint chlorination curve. Source: Metcalf et al. (2007).
At the microscopic reaction level, based on the reaction mechanism of free chlorine and
ammonia nitrogen described by Jafvert and Valentine (1992), NH3 reacts with HOCl to form
NH2Cl (Equation 1.2). NH2Cl can then react with HOCl to form NHCl2 (Equation 1.3), or
undergo a disproportionation reaction to form NHCl2 (Equation 1.5). NHCl2 can
subsequently react with HOCl to form NCl3 (Equation 1.4), or hydrolyze with water to form
an intermediate product. NH2Cl or NHCl2 can then react with the intermediate to
regenerate HOCl and other byproducts (Equations 1.6 and 1.7). Byproducts include N2,
H2O, Cl-, H+, and NO3- (Equation 1.8). NCl3 can also react with NH2Cl or NHCl2 to
regenerate HOCl (Equations 1.9 and 2.0), while HOCl further oxidizes NHCl2 to NCl3,
sustaining the catalytic reaction.
NH3 + HOCl -> NH2Cl + H2O (1.2)
NH2Cl + HOCl -> NHCl2 + H2O (1.3)
NHCl2 + HOCl -> NCl3 + H2O (1.4)
NH2Cl + NH2Cl + H+ -> NHCl2 + NH3 + H+ (1.5)


NH2Cl + intermediate I -> products (1.6)
NHCl2 + intermediate I -> HOCl + products (1.7)
2HOCl + NHCl2 + H2O -> NO3- + 5H+ + 4Cl- (1.8)
NCl3 + NH2Cl -> HOCl + products (1.9)
NCl3 + NHCl2 -> HOCl + HOCl + products (2.0)
Aghdam et al. (2021) noted that HOCl has a higher quantum yield and molar absorptivity
and can therefore generate radicals more eeectively during UV/HOCl AOP. The same paper
noted that ammonia nitrogen is commonly present in rivers, wastewater, and reclaimed
water. Municipal wastewater and reclaimed wastewater may contain approximately 1-25
mg/L ammonia nitrogen, and adding dieerent amounts of HOCl to water containing
ammonia produces chloramine species (NH2Cl, NHCl2, and NCl3) (Aghdam et al., 2021).
When UV/HOCl AOP is used to treat water containing ammonia nitrogen, NH2Cl forms
rapidly when Cl2/N molar < 1, making the process eeectively UV/NH2Cl AOP. When excess
HOCl is added (Cl2/N molar > 1.5-2), breakpoint chlorination occurs (Aghdam et al., 2021).
However, many studies of UV/breakpoint chlorination have not considered the dynamic
changes in chlorine and chloramine speciation during the process or whether irradiation
times match the reaction time for breakpoint chlorination. Therefore, dieerences between
UV/HOCl and UV/breakpoint chlorination in practical applications cannot always be
explained.
Zhang et al. (2019b) found that RO permeate from a California water-reuse plant contained
approximately 4 mg/L as Cl2 of chloramines, including approximately 2.5 mg/L as Cl2
(35.21 uM) NH2Cl and 1.5 mg/L as Cl2 (10.56 uM) NHCl2. A normal UV/HOCl operating
dose at a full-scale facility is 5-8 mg/L as Cl2 (70.4-112.7 uM) HOCl (Kwon et al., 2020;
Zhang et al., 2019b). Kintecus simulations using the Jafvert and Valentine model showed
that when 84.5 uM (~6 mg/L) HOCl was added to RO permeate under dark conditions,
HOCl and NH2Cl rapidly decreased over approximately 30 seconds to 1 minute while
NHCl2 was generated in large quantities. Around 3 minutes after the reaction began, the
species concentrations approached a stable state (Figure 1-3). Thus, NHCl2 was the main
product after breakpoint chlorination in the simulated RO permeate.


Figure 1-3. Simulated changes in species concentrations over five minutes after adding
84.5 uM HOCl to pH 5.8 RO permeate containing 35.21 uM NH2Cl and 10.56 uM NHCl2.
Full-wavelength analysis of NH2Cl, NHCl2, NCl3, HOCl, and OCl- showed that each
species has a dieerent absorbance profile. Because the first five minutes of breakpoint
chlorination are the period when chloramine concentrations change most rapidly, clarifying
the radical-generation rates of the dieerent oxidant species during this period is important
for improving UV AOP eeiciency. The average concentration of each species at 30 seconds
was multiplied by its molar absorptivity from 230-400 nm to obtain absorbance (Figure 1
4). At 254 nm, all species generally showed low absorbance. Around 290-300 nm, however,
NHCl2 showed high absorbance of about 0.011 cm^-1, indicating that NHCl2 was the
dominant absorbing species in this range.
Figure 1-4. Kintecus simulation of absorbance based on the Jafvert and Valentine model at
30 seconds of breakpoint chlorination, using the average concentrations of HOCl, OCl-,
NH2Cl, NHCl2, and NCl3 multiplied by their molar absorptivities over 230-400 nm.
Simulation conditions: pH 5.8; [NH2Cl] = 35.21 uM; [NHCl2] = 10.56 uM.
However, UV/HOCl can increase the formation of nitrosamines. Schreiber and Mitch (2007)
used dimethylamine (DMA) as a model precursor and observed N-nitrosodimethylamine
(NDMA) formation at dieerent Cl2/N molar ratios during breakpoint chlorination. NDMA
concentration increased rapidly between 1.5 < Cl2/N molar < 2 and was about six times
higher than at Cl2/N molar < 1.5 (Figure 1-5). Previous research by Bolton and Stefan found
that RO treatment removes only about 30% of NDMA (Szczuka et al., 2020). However,
NDMA has a high molar absorptivity at 254 nm, reaching 1650 M^-1 cm^-1. Thus, although
UV254 nm/breakpoint chlorination can promote NDMA formation, UV254 nm can also
photolyze NDMA eeectively (Soltermann et al., 2013).
Figure 1-5. Relationship between Cl2/N molar ratio during chlorination disinfection and
NDMA concentration. Source: Schreiber and Mitch (2007).
1.2 Research Objectives


According to Aghdam et al. (2021), in a conventional semi-enclosed laboratory reactor,
breakpoint chlorination in the dark produced little additional pollutant degradation after
about five minutes (Figure 1-6a). Researchers then investigated UV/breakpoint chlorination
for benzoic acid. Figure 1-6b shows that 35-40 minutes of irradiation increased benzoic
acid removal from 20% to 70%. One possible reason is the geometry of the conventional
laboratory-scale semi-enclosed reactor. To produce partially collimated light, a distance is
maintained between the UV source and the water surface (Figure 1-7), reducing the light
intensity reaching the water and requiring a longer irradiation period. More importantly, the
results may depend on how long dieerent species have been reacting, which can change
oxidant forms and the composition of disinfection byproducts (DBPs).
Kwon et al. (2020) reported that a pilot-scale AOP system can provide approximately 700
1000 mJ/cm2 of UV dose within about one minute, which more closely matches breakpoint
chlorination reaction times and practical hydraulic retention times in treatment plants.
Figure 1-6. Eeect of initial chlorine dosage on benzoic-acid degradation during (a)
breakpoint chlorination in the dark and (b) UV254 nm/breakpoint chlorination. Conditions:
pH 7; [benzoic acid]0 = 5 uM; [N-NH3]0 = 1 mg/L; [HOCl]0 = 7.6, 9.6, 12.6, and 17.6 mg/L as
Cl2. Source: Aghdam et al. (2021).
Figure 1-7. Schematic of a conventional semi-enclosed laboratory-scale reactor.
Based on these findings and the simulations, the study proposed two hypotheses.
Hypothesis 1: changing the light source from the commonly used 254 nm wavelength to a
UVB source around 300 nm could transfer energy more eeectively to dichloramine,
allowing more eeicient radical generation and improved pollutant degradation. Hypothesis
2: compared with UV/HOCl and UV/chlorine, using a UVB light source together with
breakpoint chlorination as an AOP in a water-reuse treatment system may reduce NDMA
and other nitrogenous disinfection byproducts.
This study therefore investigates pollutant degradation using UVB306 nm with breakpoint
chlorination, examines whether UVB irradiation promotes radical generation by
dichloramine, and compares the process with UV254 nm/breakpoint chlorination and


UV/H2O2. The study also seeks optimized operating conditions for the UVB306
nm/breakpoint chlorination AOP and examines NDMA formation and degradation under the
optimized conditions.
