2.1 Wastewater Reuse
2.1.1 Purpose and Methods of Wastewater Reuse
Recycling and reusing wastewater can reduce demand for freshwater resources, relieve
water stress, and provide sustainable water supplies in water-scarce regions. It can also
reduce withdrawals from rivers, lakes, and groundwater, thereby helping protect aquatic
ecosystems and improve water quality.
The main objectives of wastewater reuse can be achieved with a full advanced treatment
(FAT) process (Gerrity et al., 2013). Wastewater is treated using membranes and advanced
oxidation as physical and chemical barriers and is then, depending on the reuse purpose,
discharged to engineered bueer reservoirs or environmental bueer water bodies, thereby
improving contaminant removal and water quality.
2.1.2 Wastewater Treatment Process in a FAT System
As shown in Figure 2-1, the main units of the FAT system are membrane processes such as
MF/UF and RO, together with a UV/H2O2 advanced oxidation process. MF/UF and RO use
physical filtration to remove particles, microorganisms, and dissolved substances. The
principal technologies are described below.


1. Microfiltration (MF):
Microfiltration is a physical filtration process that directly filters particles, bacteria,
suspended solids, and large organic molecules through micropores. Typical pore sizes are
0.1-10 micrometers, so MF cannot eeectively remove smaller dissolved substances.
2. Ultrafiltration (UF):
Ultrafiltration is a further physical filtration process capable of eeectively removing
microorganisms, viruses, organic molecules, colloids, and macromolecular substances
while retaining dissolved inorganic substances. Typical UF pore sizes are 0.001-0.1
micrometers, smaller than those of MF. UF is widely used in drinking-water treatment,
industrial water treatment, and wastewater reuse to provide high-quality water.
3. Reverse Osmosis (RO):
Reverse osmosis is a pressure-driven membrane filtration process. Water is forced under
high pressure through a semipermeable membrane, removing dissolved solutes, salts,
organic compounds, and microorganisms. RO is not eeective at removing small neutral
molecules (Gerrity et al., 2013). RO membrane pores are typically about 0.0001-0.001
micrometers, allowing removal of almost all dissolved substances and microorganisms.
RO is widely used for drinking-water treatment, seawater desalination, industrial water,
and wastewater reuse to produce high-quality water with low dissolved-solids content.
In a typical FAT wastewater-reuse system, secondary and tertiary eeluent is collected and
treated with a high concentration of HOCl. Ammonia in the eeluent reacts with added
HOCl to form chloramines, mainly monochloramine and dichloramine, thereby controlling
biological growth in downstream membrane processes. Because small neutral molecules
are not easily removed by RO, chloramines ultimately appear in the RO permeate and enter
the UV reactor. Conventionally, H2O2 is used as the oxidant in the UV reactor for advanced
oxidation. After stabilization, the treated reclaimed water can be supplied to a water
treatment plant as an alternative source.
Figure 2-1. FAT treatment flow for wastewater reuse. Source: Gerrity et al. (2013).


2.2 Advanced Oxidation Processes
Advanced oxidation processes (AOPs) are advanced technologies used in water and
wastewater treatment, primarily to remove organic pollutants, heavy metals, and other
harmful substances. AOPs generally use oxidants to alter or destroy pollutant molecular
structures, converting them into harmless substances or forms that are easier to treat
(Fang et al., 2014). Oxidants can generate radicals more eeiciently under irradiation,
creating processes such as UV/H2O2 and improving contaminant removal. AOPs are
widely used in industrial wastewater treatment, drinking-water treatment, and groundwater
remediation.
2.2.1 Characteristics of Advanced Oxidation Processes
Three common characteristics of AOP technologies are: (1) AOPs use highly reactive
oxidants such as ozone (O3) and hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) to oxidize or destroy the
chemical structures of organic pollutants; (2) AOPs may use ultraviolet or visible light to
activate oxidants and promote pollutant degradation; and (3) ozonation itself is a form of
AOP in which ozone gas oxidizes contaminants in water and converts them into
compounds that are easier to treat.
Based on previous UV/H2O2 studies, the eeectiveness of an AOP depends in part on the
steady-state concentration of radicals in the system. After radicals are generated, they
react with substances in the system and are continually generated and consumed, creating
a dynamic balance that maintains a stable concentration (Chuang and Shi, 2022). Equation
2.1 shows that radical steady-state concentration depends on the oxidant molar
absorptivity (epsilon), quantum yield (Phi) (Yin et al., 2020), pollutant concentration, and
intermediates. If the radical production rate exceeds the radical scavenging rate, radical
steady-state concentration increases and pollutant removal can improve.
