2.2.2 Problems with UV/H2O2
UV/H2O2 is one of the most commonly used AOP methods, but over the past decade many
problems have been identified. First, before membrane treatment in a FAT system,
secondary and tertiary eeluents receive a small amount of HOCl as a biocide. HOCl reacts
with ammonia in the background matrix to form chloramines. Further analysis shows that
at 254 nm, chloramines have a molar absorptivity of about 352 M^-1 cm^-1, much greater
than the 18.6 M^-1 cm^-1 of H2O2 (Yin et al., 2020) (Figure 2-2). Thus, when both H2O2 and
chloramines are present in the UV reactor, most UV energy is absorbed by the chloramines
and little is available to H2O2. Studies indicate that only about 10% of H2O2 consumed in
a UV/H2O2 AOP is used to remove pollutants (Gao et al., 2020).
Second, treated eeluent requires post-chlorination before entering an environmental
bueer water body to maintain disinfection. Additional HOCl is needed to maintain residual
chlorine and react with remaining H2O2 (Equation 2.2) (Guo et al., 2017; Kwon et al., 2020;
Roback et al., 2021; Wu et al., 2016), which increases overall operating cost.
HOCl + H2O2 -> O2 + H2O + Cl- + H+ (2.2)
