2.3 Breakpoint Chlorination Reaction
2.3.1 Reaction Mechanism of Breakpoint Chlorination
Free residual chlorine refers to active chlorine species existing in water as hypochlorous
acid (HOCl) and hypochlorite ion (OCl-) (Equations 2.6-2.8). Their relative proportions
depend on pH; at lower pH, the proportion of HOCl is higher. Both HOCl and OCl- are
strong oxidants and can damage microbial cell membranes and intracellular proteins and
other biological macromolecules.
Cl- + H2O -> HOCl + OH- (2.6)
Cl2 + H2O -> HCl + HOCl (2.7)
HOCl -> H+ + ClO- (2.8)
When free ammonia (ammonium [NH4+] and ammonia [NH3]) and free residual chlorine
(HOCl and OCl-) coexist in water, three inorganic chloramines can form: monochloramine
(NH2Cl), dichloramine (NHCl2), and trichloramine (NCl3) (Equations 2.9-2.11).
NH3 + HOCl -> NH2Cl + H2O (2.9)
NH2Cl + HOCl -> NHCl2 + H2O (2.10)


NHCl2 + HOCl -> NCl3 + H2O (2.11)
Secondary and tertiary eeluents in water-reuse processes contain free ammonia. If water
contains a certain amount of ammonia nitrogen and dieerent amounts of free residual
chlorine are added according to the chlorine-to-nitrogen molar ratio (Cl2/N molar), the
breakpoint chlorination curve shown in Figure 2-3 can be obtained after approximately 30
minutes.
Breakpoint chlorination generally consists of three stages. Before a chlorine-to-nitrogen
mass ratio of 5:1 (Cl2/N molar = 1:1), free residual chlorine reacts with ammonia nitrogen
through a series of complex reactions, mainly forming monochloramine. As free chlorine
increases, monochloramine and total chlorine concentrations also increase. When Cl2/N
molar reaches 1:1, free residual chlorine begins to consume combined residual chlorine
until the breakpoint, at which point Cl2/N molar is about 1.5:1-2:1 (Metcalf et al., 2007;
Wahman, 2018).
The breakpoint is the point at which total chlorine concentration is lowest. Dichloramine is
the main species before the breakpoint. After the breakpoint, total chlorine concentration
remains relatively stable because ammonia has been consumed and additional free
chlorine remains in the water as free residual chlorine (Jafvert and Valentine, 1992).
