2.3.2 Comparison of Radical Generation by Monochloramine and Dichloramine
When free residual chlorine and ammonia nitrogen coexist, breakpoint chlorination occurs.
The main sources of radicals are monochloramine and dichloramine. Radical generation
depends on the oxidant's molar absorptivity (epsilon) and quantum yield (Phi), and the
resulting radical concentration aeects pollutant degradation. Molar absorptivity describes
the ability of an oxidant to absorb light at a particular wavelength, while quantum yield


describes the number of radicals generated after the oxidant absorbs light (Chuang and
Shi, 2022).
According to Yin et al. (2020), the molar absorptivities of NH2Cl at 255, 265, 285, and 300
nm are 345, 174, 75, and 4 M^-1 cm^-1, respectively. The corresponding values for NHCl2
are 96, 111, 214, and 261 M^-1 cm^-1. Table 2-2 gives apparent and intrinsic quantum
yields. Because radical generation depends on both molar absorptivity and quantum yield,
their product provides a better comparison of radical generation at a given wavelength. The
results in Table 2-3 show that at 255 nm, NH2Cl has a higher product of intrinsic quantum
yield and molar absorptivity than NHCl2. At 265, 285, and 300 nm, NHCl2 has the higher
product. Thus, 255 nm favors radical generation from NH2Cl, while the 280-320 nm UVB
range favors radical generation from NHCl2. Therefore, the primary objective of this
research is to create more dichloramine in the system so that dichloramine can generate
large quantities of radicals under UVB irradiation, thereby improving UV AOP performance
and ultimately enabling application in water-reuse drinking-water systems.
