2.4 Application of Breakpoint Chlorination in Water Reuse Systems
2.4.1 Simulation of Chloramine Species Concentrations in RO Permeate Using a
Mathematical Model
Over the past decade, researchers have studied UV/HOCl and UV/chloramine as
replacements for UV/H2O2. When chloramines are present in the RO permeate of a water
reuse system, added HOCl undergoes breakpoint chlorination with them. Zhang et al.
(2019b) found that RO permeate from a California water-reuse facility contained
approximately 4 mg/L as Cl2 chloramines, including 2.5 mg/L as Cl2 (35.21 uM) NH2Cl and
1.5 mg/L as Cl2 (10.56 uM) NHCl2. A typical full-scale UV/HOCl dose is 5-8 mg/L as Cl2
(70.4-112.7 uM) HOCl.
Kintecus simulations based on Jafvert and Valentine (1992) were conducted at pH 5.8 with
background concentrations of 35.21 uM NH2Cl and 10.56 uM NHCl2, adding 84.5 uM (~6
mg/L) HOCl. Under dark conditions, HOCl and NH2Cl rapidly decreased over
approximately 30 seconds to 1 minute, while a large amount of NHCl2 formed (Figure 2
5a). After about 3 minutes, the concentrations of the species approached equilibrium,
showing that NHCl2 is the major product after breakpoint chlorination in RO permeate.
When comparing systems with 84.5 uM (~6 mg/L) versus 112.7 uM (~8 mg/L) HOCl, the
concentration changes during the first 30 seconds to 1 minute were very similar. At later
times, increasing HOCl did not produce more NHCl2; instead, more HOCl remained in the
system and NHCl2 decreased slightly after 4-5 minutes. NCl3 remained below 1 uM in both
systems because it reacts very rapidly with NHCl2 (F12, kF12 = 5.6x10^3 M^-1 s^-1) and is
therefore rapidly consumed after formation.


