2.5 Relationship Between Radicals Generated during Breakpoint Chlorination and Pollutant
Removal
Based on the structures and properties of six model micropollutants - benzoic acid (BA),
nitrobenzene (NB), caeeine, carbamazepine (CBZ), 1,4-dioxane, and N,N-diethyl-m
toluamide (DEET) - and their reaction-rate constants with reactive species that may be
generated during photolysis of free chlorine and chloramines, including •OH, •Cl, •ClO,
and •Cl2- (Patton et al., 2018; Patton et al., 2022; Zhang et al., 2019a), the six pollutants
compete for dieerent radicals. Therefore, selecting pollutants with dieerent reactivities
toward •OH, •Cl, •ClO, and •Cl2- provides a useful set of indicators for the study.
For example, nitrobenzene is highly reactive toward •OH (k•OH = 3.9x10^9 M^-1 s^-1)
(Aghdam et al., 2021), but there is insueicient literature on its reaction with chlorine
radicals to predict its actual degradation in water containing other pollutants. 1,4-dioxane
is highly reactive toward •OH (k•OH = 3.1x10^9 M^-1 s^-1) (Patton et al., 2017) but is less
reactive toward •Cl (k•Cl = 4.4x10^6 M^-1 s^-1). DEET has high reaction-rate constants
toward both •OH (4.6x10^9 M^-1 s^-1) and •Cl (3.8x10^9 M^-1 s^-1) (Sun et al., 2016), so
when 1,4-dioxane and DEET are present together, DEET preferentially reacts with •Cl and
competes with 1,4-dioxane for •OH.
Caeeine and carbamazepine also react with •ClO and •Cl2-, in addition to •OH and •Cl.
Their reported constants include k•OH = 6.4x10^9 M^-1 s^-1 for caeeine and 8.8x10^9 M^
1 s^-1 for carbamazepine; k•Cl = 1.5x10^10 M^-1 s^-1 for caeeine and 5.6x10^10 M^-1 s^-1
for carbamazepine (Guo et al., 2017; Lei et al., 2019; Wang et al., 2016).


The reaction-rate constants indicate that •OH preferentially reacts with caeeine,
carbamazepine, and DEET; •Cl preferentially reacts with benzoic acid, caeeine,
carbamazepine, and DEET; and •ClO and •Cl2- preferentially react with caeeine and
carbamazepine. Literature also indicates that nitrobenzene and benzoic acid are not
readily degraded by UV but are highly reactive with radicals, so dieerent UV energy
densities do not strongly aeect their radical reaction rates (Aghdam et al., 2021).
Water-reuse systems contain many unknown contaminants. Because contaminants
compete for dieerent radicals, selecting appropriate target pollutants while considering
pollutant-radical rate constants is important for making laboratory results more
representative of actual waters. Previous studies have generally analyzed only two or three
pollutants and have less often considered systematic competition among multiple
pollutants. The six pollutants selected here have dieerent structures and radical
reactivities and therefore better represent the range of behavior expected in real water.
