2.6 Formation and Degradation of NDMA under Breakpoint Chlorination


According to Szczuka and Mitch et al. (2020), the concentration of nitrosamines increases
significantly after RO permeate in a water-reuse system undergoes UV/HOCl AOP. One
proposed NDMA formation pathway under breakpoint chlorination is hydrolysis of NHCl2
to nitroxyl/hyponitrous intermediates, followed by reaction of HNO with HOCl to produce
nitrosyl chloride (ClNO), which then reacts with dimethylamine (DMA) to form NDMA
(Equations 2.12 and 2.13) (Szczuka et al., 2020).
NHCl2 + H2O -> HNO / ClNO (2.12)
ClNO + DMA -> NDMA + HCl (2.13)
In 2021, Pham and Fairey et al. re-examined the NDMA pathway proposed by Szczuka and
Mitch. They proposed that NHCl2 hydrolyzes to HNO, which then reacts with oxygen to
form peroxynitrous acid (HOONO) or peroxynitrite (OONO-) (Equation 2.14). These species
then react with DMA to produce NDMA (Equation 2.15). Their work identified another
possible NDMA pathway and emphasized the importance of HOONO and OONO- during
UV/HOCl treatment (Pham et al., 2021).
NHCl2 + H2O -> HNO / NO• -> HOONO / OONO- (2.14)
HOONO / OONO- + DMA -> NDMA (2.15)
Because these two groups of researchers proposed somewhat dieerent theories for NDMA
formation, Chuang et al. (2023) further investigated whether the HOCl-HNO pathway
proposed by Szczuka et al. was related to reactions between NCl3 and NHCl2. Their results
indicated that NCl3 plays an important role in increasing NDMA formation during
breakpoint chlorination because NHCl2 and NCl3 can react with each other to generate
NDMA, while NCl3 forms through the reaction of HOCl and NHCl2.
NHCl2 + NCl3 -> NDMA + other byproducts (2.16)
NHCl2 + HOCl -> NCl3 + H2O (2.17)


The study proposed two ways to reduce NDMA. First, when Cl2/N molar < 2, the
accumulated ClNO concentration after one hour decreases as Cl2/N molar increases
(Figure 2-12). This indicates that excess HOCl and OCl- convert NHCl2 to NCl3, lower the
NHCl2 concentration, reduce the mutual reaction of NCl3 and NHCl2, and thereby reduce
NDMA formation. Second, NHCl2 and NCl3 are readily photolyzed in a UV reactor, which
can help control nitrosamine formation during breakpoint chlorination.
Previous studies indicate that RO membranes remove only about 30% of NDMA (Szczuka et
al., 2020). NDMA, however, has a molar absorptivity at 254 nm of 1650 M^-1 cm^-1.
Although UV/breakpoint chlorination promotes NDMA formation, it can also rapidly
photolyze NDMA. Therefore, using an appropriate energy density to control NDMA
generated during the process is important, particularly in water-reuse systems intended for
potable use (McCurry et al., 2017). This study therefore examines whether breakpoint
chlorination with a UVB light source can achieve eeects comparable to 254 nm.
