available annular stainless-steel reactor was selected. The study used an 8 W UVC254 nm
light source and 4 W, 6 W, and 8 W UVB306 nm light sources. Because lamp energy dieers
with lamp wattage, photon-flux experiments were first conducted to determine the photon
energy emitted per unit time (s), expressed as joules (J), at 254 and 306 nm for dieerent
lamp powers. This allowed the experimental results to be compared more appropriately.
Stage 2: UV/breakpoint chlorination experiments in pH 5.8 and pH 7 phosphate bueers
Breakpoint chlorination pollutant-degradation experiments were conducted in phosphate
bueered solutions at pH 5.8 and pH 7. UVB irradiation was used to excite chloramine
species generated during breakpoint chlorination and produce radicals. The study
examined whether UVB can eeectively generate radicals for pollutant removal and
compared pollutant degradation rates and changes in total chlorine concentration during
the reaction.
Stage 3: UV/breakpoint chlorination experiments in synthetic RO permeate
The research target was RO permeate from a wastewater-reuse treatment plant after
membrane treatment. Because actual RO permeate is dieicult to obtain, synthetic RO
permeate was prepared in the laboratory. The study investigated pollutant degradation
rates and changes in total chlorine during UV/breakpoint chlorination under dieerent Cl2/N
molar ratios, background chloramine concentrations, lamp powers, and reactor reflective
surface materials.
Stage 4: Comparison of NDMA photolysis under UVC254 nm and UVB306 nm
This stage compared the rate of NDMA photolysis and the rate of NDMA formation after
reactions between dichloramine and trichloramine under UVC254 nm and UVB306 nm with
breakpoint chlorination. The main question was whether NDMA can also be eeectively
photolyzed under a UVB light source.


