3.3 Experimental Methods
3.3.1 Photon Flux Measurement Method for the Annular Reactor
According to Goldstein and Rabani (2008), the potassium iodide-potassium iodate method
has greater than 99.9% light absorption below 285 nm. According to Lee et al. (2007), the
ferrioxalate method is suitable in the 300-390 nm range. Therefore, the ferrioxalate
chemical actinometry method was selected for measuring photon flux in the UVB range.
During the experiment, 0.02 M ammonium ferric sulfate dodecahydrate was dissolved in
400 mL of 0.1 N H2SO4, and 0.06 M potassium oxalate was dissolved in another 400 mL of
0.1 N H2SO4. The two solutions were combined in an aluminum-foil-covered beaker, giving
a total volume of 800 mL. In the absence of irradiation, the mixed solution was pumped
through the annular stainless-steel reactor at 2400 mL/min. The lamp was then switched
on while the pump and timer were started simultaneously. Under 306 nm UVB irradiation,
samples were collected every 30 seconds, 1.5 mL each, and 1.5 mL of 0.1% (w/v) 1,10
phenanthroline solution was added. Samples were allowed to react in the dark for 30
minutes, after which absorbance of Fe(1,10-phen)3^2+ was measured at 510 nm using a
UV-Vis spectrophotometer. The emitted photon energy per unit time (J/s) was then
calculated.


3.3.2 Micropollutant Degradation Method
To investigate the eeects of water pH and Cl2/N molar on pollutant degradation, 10 mM
phosphate-bueer solutions at pH 5.8 and pH 7 were prepared. Because ascorbic acid
undergoes a redox reaction with HOCl and deactivates chlorine, ascorbic acid could be
used before sample analysis to terminate breakpoint chlorination and ensure accurate
pollutant measurements.
Experimental procedure:
1. Warm up the UV lamp for 30 minutes.
2. To 850 mL of 10 mM phosphate bueer at pH 5.8 or pH 7, add 0.5 uM each of
nitrobenzene, benzoic acid, CBZ, and caeeine, and 0.2 uM each of 1,4-dioxane and DEET.
Mix thoroughly, then add 50 uM NH4Cl.
3. With the lamp oe, pump the mixed solution through the annular stainless-steel reactor
at 2400 mL/min.
4. Add 100, 125, or 150 uM HOCl to the beaker outside the reactor, corresponding to Cl2/N
molar = 2, 2.5, or 3. Immediately after adding HOCl, switch on the lamp and start the timer.
5. Collect samples at specified times and store them in 20 mL vials containing ascorbic
acid at 1.1 times the total chlorine concentration.
Total chlorine was analyzed during the experiment using the N,N-diethyl-p
phenylenediamine (DPD) colorimetric method, based on APHA Standard Method 4500-Cl
G.
Analysis procedure:
1. Add 150 uL of 385 mM pH 6.5 phosphate bueer to 3 mL of sample.
2. Add 150 uL of 3.81 mM DPD indicator.
3. Add approximately 1 g of potassium iodide powder.


4. Mix thoroughly and allow to react for 2 minutes.
5. Measure absorbance at 515 nm using a UV-Vis spectrophotometer.
After the experiment, pollutant concentrations were analyzed. Dieerent instruments
required the following procedures:
1. Transfer 1 mL of the 10 mL sample to a small vial and analyze nitrobenzene, benzoic
acid, CBZ, and caeeine using HPLC-UV.
2. Add 300 uL of 0.514 uM 1,4-dioxane-d8 to the remaining 9 mL as the internal standard
for 1,4-dioxane.
3. Add 2 mL methyl tert-butyl ether and 12 g Na2SO4, shake, and allow to stand for 2
minutes for liquid-liquid extraction (LLE).
4. Transfer the upper organic layer to a small vial using a glass dropper and analyze 1,4
dioxane and DEET by GC-MS.
3.3.3 Preparation of Synthetic RO Permeate
To investigate RO permeate from actual water-reuse facilities, which is dieicult to obtain,
experiments were conducted using laboratory-synthesized RO permeate. The synthetic
water was then used for UV/breakpoint chlorination AOP experiments at dieerent chlorine
to-nitrogen molar ratios.
The composition of the synthetic RO permeate was mainly based on Mangalgiri et al.
(2019) and Zhang et al. (2019a). Literature indicates that RO permeate in potable-reuse
systems commonly contains carbonate (~0.5 mM), DOC (~0.2 mg/L), chloramines (28.2
42.3 uM NH2Cl and 9.2-14.1 uM NHCl2), chloride, and inorganic nitrogen species that
scavenge radicals, such as NO3-. The study therefore added these components to 2 mM
phosphate-bueered deionized water at pH 5.6 to prepare synthetic RO permeate. Literature
reports a k•OH of 2.1-2.5x10^4 m (mg-C/L)^-1 s^-1 for DOC in RO permeate, so a mixture
of 77% acetone and 23% methanol on a molar-carbon basis was used to represent the
DOC contribution. The resulting synthetic RO permeate had k•OH of approximately
2.1x10^4 (mg-C/L)^-1 s^-1.


