4.1 Photon Flux Results for the Annular Reactor
The lamps used in the study were: (1) 8 W, 254 nm; (2) 4 W, 306 nm; (3) 6 W, 306 nm; and (4)
8 W, 306 nm. Photon-flux experiments were first performed to determine the photon energy
emitted per unit time in the stainless-steel annular reactor.
Photon flux was determined by measuring the molar absorptivity of Fe(1,10-phen)3^2+ at
510 nm and calculating its complex concentration using epsilon510 nm = 11,100 M^-1
cm^-1. Plotting complex concentration against time gives a linear relationship. The slope of
concentration versus time was used to calculate photons emitted per second. Multiplying
the photon emission rate by the energy contained in one mole of Einstein photons at the


specified wavelength gives the photon flux, expressed as photon energy emitted per unit
time (J/s) (Equations 4.1-4.2).
Photon energy emitted (J/s) = (Slope x V / QY) x U (4.1)
where V is solution volume (L); QY is quantum yield. According to Goldstein and Rabani
(2008), QY = 1.40 for UVC254 nm and 1.25 for UVB306 nm.
U(lambda) = (hc/lambda) x 6.02 x 10^23 x 10^3
= (6.626x10^-34 x 2.998x10^8 / lambda) x 6.02x10^23 x 10^3 (4.2)
where U is the energy contained in one mole of Einstein photons at the specified
wavelength (mJ/mole Einsteins); h is Planck's constant (6.626x10^-34 m2 kg/s); and c is the
speed of light (2.998x10^8 m/s).
The ferrioxalate method was used to measure photon flux from UVC254 nm and UVB306
nm lamps of dieerent wattages. The calculated photon energies emitted per unit time are
summarized in Table 4-1 and compared with manufacturer information (Figures 4-1 and 4
2).
