4.3 Comparison of UV/Breakpoint Chlorination and UV/H2O2 for Degradation of Model
Pollutants in Phosphate Bueer
UV/H2O2 is a mature AOP commonly used in water treatment plants for controlling organic
pollutants in drinking-water sources and RO units in water reuse (Kwon et al., 2020).
However, H2O2 has low photon-utilization eeiciency and may remain in the treated
eeluent, increasing disinfection costs.
The initial hypothesis of this study was to use a laboratory-scale stainless-steel reactor to
conduct UVC254 nm/breakpoint chlorination and UVB306 nm/breakpoint chlorination
pollutant-degradation experiments, using 8 W UVC254 nm with 3 mg/L H2O2 as the
comparison baseline to determine whether UV/breakpoint chlorination could increase AOP
eeiciency.
In kinetic studies, ln([C]/[C]0) = -kobs x t is commonly used to describe first-order reactant
concentration changes. When ln([C]/[C]0) varies linearly with time, the slope is -kobs,
where kobs is a pseudo-first-order degradation rate constant with units of s^-1 or min^-1. It
can be used to compare pollutant degradation. Because lamps of dieerent wattages
produce dieerent photon fluxes during the same irradiation time, direct comparison by
time is not appropriate. Therefore, experimental results were plotted as ln([C]/[C]0) versus
photon energy emitted per unit time (J/s).
4.3.1 Comparison of UVB306 nm/Breakpoint Chlorination under Weakly Acidic and Neutral
Conditions


Previous studies indicate that when pH changes from weakly acidic to neutral,
intermediates formed by oxidant or free-chlorine decomposition can increase radical
scavenging and lower pollutant-removal eeiciency in UV/breakpoint chlorination AOPs
(Chuang et al., 2017; Zhang et al., 2019b). Therefore, phosphate bueers at pH 5.8 and pH 7
were used to investigate the eeect of pH on pollutant degradation during breakpoint
chlorination under irradiation.
Experiments were first conducted using 8 W UVB306 nm with Cl2/N molar = 2 to degrade
nitrobenzene and 1,4-dioxane at dieerent pH values, and the results were compared with 8
W UVC254 nm and 3 mg/L H2O2. For nitrobenzene, kobs at pH 5.8 and pH 7 under 8 W
UVB306 nm/breakpoint chlorination were 0.0019 and 0.0028 J^-1, respectively. For 1,4
dioxane, kobs values were 0.0013 and 0.0025 J^-1, respectively.
Under weakly acidic conditions (pH 5.8), pollutant degradation removal was more than
32% greater than under neutral conditions (pH 7) for both nitrobenzene and 1,4-dioxane,
consistent with the literature. In addition, under weakly acidic conditions, 8 W UVB306
nm/breakpoint chlorination produced pollutant-removal performance about 1.2 times that
of 8 W UVC254 nm/H2O2. This indicates feasibility of UVB306 nm/breakpoint chlorination
in a weakly acidic environment.
