4.6.3 Eeect of Changing Reactor Reflective Surface Material on Pollutant Degradation
The reactor used in this study had a stainless steel reflective surface. According to the
literature (Kim et al., 2004), stainless steel has a light reflectance of 20%-30%. Haefner et
al. (2017) reported that Teflon (polytetrafluoroethylene, PTFE) has excellent light
reflectance of 90%-100%. Because reflective surface material may aeect the amount of
light reaching the water per unit time and therefore aeect dichloramine photon utilization,
the study tested UV/breakpoint chlorination using reactors with three reflective surface
materials: glass, stainless steel, and Teflon. Figure 4-20 shows the reactor dimensions and
the dieerent reflective materials. For the Teflon reactor, a 0.5 mm thick Teflon sheet was
attached to the inside of a glass reactor (Figures 4-20b and 4-21).
Teflon is composed of repeating tetrafluoroethylene monomers. The highly electronegative
fluorine atoms produce strong molecular bonding, reducing surface defects and
absorption and increasing light reflectance. Using Teflon in lamps or reflector panels can
improve photon utilization eeiciency and reduce energy consumption. Glass, with a light
reflectance of 4%-8%, is transparent, strong, chemically resistant, and easy to clean and is
widely used in buildings, household products, electronic products, and transportation.
However, because its surface is relatively rough and its refractive index is high, its ability to
reflect light is relatively poor.
