CHAPTER 5. CONCLUSIONS AND RECOMMENDATIONS
5.1 Conclusions
This study investigated the eeects of UVB306 nm/breakpoint chlorination AOP on pollutant
degradation and tested optimal operating conditions. The following conclusions were
obtained:
1. The steady-state concentration of radicals has a major eeect on pollutant removal.
Compared with neutral conditions, weakly acidic conditions (pH 5.8) help increase radical
stability and thereby improve pollutant removal by UV AOP.
2. After adding dieerent HOCl concentrations to synthetic RO permeate and measuring
total chlorine, the breakpoint occurred at Cl2/N molar = 2-2.25. Experiments with dieerent
chlorine dosages under 8 W UVB306 nm/breakpoint chlorination showed that chlorine
addition above the breakpoint improved pollutant removal. In particular, at Cl2/N molar =
2.72, similar pollutant degradation rates to other chlorine-to-nitrogen ratios could be
achieved with less energy.
3. Increasing the background chloramine concentration in synthetic RO permeate did not
significantly aeect pollutant degradation.
4. Experiments investigating optimal operating conditions for UVB306 nm/breakpoint
chlorination AOP showed that reducing the light energy supplied per unit time can create


more dichloramine in the system. When dichloramine absorbs 306 nm light, more radicals
(•Cl) can be generated. Using highly reflective Teflon as the reactor reflective surface also
reduces photon-energy loss and increases dichloramine photon utilization per unit time.
Therefore, breakpoint chlorination with 4 W UVB306 nm in a Teflon reactor can increase the
ability of UV AOP to degrade pollutants in water.
5. Comparison of UV/H2O2 AOP, 8 W UVC254 nm/breakpoint chlorination AOP, and
optimized UVB306 nm/breakpoint chlorination AOP, together with the pollutant
degradation and NDMA photolysis results, indicated that breakpoint chlorination with 4 W
UVB306 nm in a Teflon reactor has potential for future wastewater reuse applications for
potable water. However, application requires further consideration of whether UVB306
nm/breakpoint chlorination AOP generates more disinfection byproducts or causes greater
toxicity.
5.2 Recommendations
1. The experiments in this study involved changes in many nitrogen-containing species,
including ammonia nitrogen, chloramine, nitrate nitrogen, and nitrite nitrogen. Total
nitrogen (TN) is an important water-quality parameter that can be used to evaluate the
nutrient status and degree of pollution of a water body. Future experiments are therefore
recommended to use a TOC instrument to measure changes in TN.
2. Future research can further investigate whether UVB306 nm/breakpoint chlorination
AOP promotes the formation of nitrogen-containing disinfection byproducts compared
with UVC254 nm/breakpoint chlorination AOP.
3. During wastewater reuse treatment, oxidants inevitably react with organic and inorganic
substances in the water and produce disinfection byproducts (DBPs). These DBPs can
cause ecological toxicity, persistence, and bioaccumulation and may pose developmental,
acute, and carcinogenic risks to human health. Before UVB/breakpoint chlorination AOP is
applied at full scale, further experiments should analyze additional DBP species and
conduct toxicity tests to determine whether more DBPs are generated or greater toxicity
occurs.


